erm environment, though a Republican-sponsored poll shows the race even. Markets price her near two-in-three while most raters still call it a Tossup, so a narrow Democratic edge with wide uncertainty fits the evidence best.", "watch": "Ramaswamy's $30 million war chest could saturate Ohio airwaves in the final weeks, or a late national shift toward Republicans could erase Acton's narrow lead.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Open seat: Mike DeWine (R) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "Ohio's governor race is an open-seat contest between Democrat Amy Acton and Republican Vivek Ramaswamy, with term-limited Mike DeWine leaving office. Polling through early October shows a narrow Democratic edge: a New York Times/Siena survey put Acton up four, Marist up six, and Suffolk up nine, while a Republican-sponsored Big Data poll had the race even. Averages of the polls place Acton ahead by roughly three to five points. Six of seven expert raters call the race a Tossup, with one calling it Lean Democratic.\n\nRamaswamy holds a large financial advantage, with about $30.7 million on hand to Acton's $8.1 million, and Ohio's electorate leans Republican by about five points. But the midterm environment has favored Democrats, and prediction markets price Acton's chances near two in three. Headlines describe a bitter race and note Ramaswamy's struggles in a state his party usually carries. A small Democratic lead with wide uncertainty best fits the evidence.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.545, "margin": 0.49}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.5929, "margin": 1.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.5919, "margin": 1.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.6166, "margin": 1.3}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.5684, "margin": 1.2}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.592, "margin": 1.4}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.6233, "margin": 1.5}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.6597, "margin": 2.0}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.662, "margin": 2.0}, {"date": "